        "caption": "A Caterpillar wheel loader is secured onto a truck at Ideal Tractor in West Sacramento, California, US, on Monday, Aug. 1, 2022. Caterpillar Inc. is scheduled to release earnings figures on August 2. ",
        "credit": "David Paul MorrisBloomberg via Getty Images",
        "__typename": "rankingListItemImage"
      },
      "itemDescription": "The 100-year-old construction equipment company rose five spots in 2023 from No. 73 last year. In the 2022 fiscal year, Caterpillar\u2019s sales and revenue rose 16.6% to $59.4 billion, marking one of the best years in company history.<br />\r\n<ul><br />\r\n \t<li>Caterpillar moved its global headquarters from Deerfield, Ill., to Irving, Texas, in 2022.</li><br />\r\n \t<li>To cope with inflation and increasing costs, Caterpillar raised its equipment prices, which helped boost its sales.</li><br />\r\n \t<li>But the heavy-machinery maker still had its fair share of struggles in 2022 in the form of COVID-era supply-chain snarls, inflationary forces, and slow pickup in Chinese demand.</li><br />\r\n \t<li>In October 2022, Caterpillar waived an internal policy that would have required CEO Jim Umpleby to retire when he turns 65, allowing him to continue as the top executive.</li><br />\r\n \t<li>The company is the world\u2019s leading manufacturer of construction and mining equipment, off-highway diesel and natural gas engines, industrial gas turbines, and diesel-electric locomotives, and now has roughly 60% of its operations outside the U.S.</li><br />\r\n</ul>",
